(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_thermalpropertiesofmatter=[{id:"neet-2026r-q039",pn:39,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal expansion of volume",q:"The temperature of a metallic sphere of radius R is increased by a small amount \u0394T. If the linear coefficient of thermal expansion of the metal is \u03B1, the approximate increase in the volume of the sphere is:",o:["3\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","4\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","6\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T","2\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T"],c:1,s:`For the metallic sphere, \u0394R/R = \u03B1\u0394T.
Volume of the sphere V = (4)/(3)\u03C0R\xB3.
So \u0394V/V = 3\u0394R/R.
Increase in volume \u0394V = 3(\u0394R/R)((4)/(3)\u03C0R\xB3) = 4\u03C0R\xB3\u03B1\u0394T.`},{id:"neet-2025-q019",pn:19,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Heat conduction in series",q:"Three identical heat conducting rods are connected in series as shown in the figure. The rods on the sides have thermal conductivity 2K while that in the middle has thermal conductivity K. The left end of the combination is maintained at temperature 3T and the right end at T. The rods are thermally insulated from outside. In steady state, temperature at the left junction is T\u2081 and that at the right junction is T\u2082. The ratio T\u2081/T\u2082 is",o:["3/2","4/3","5/3","5/4"],c:2,s:`In series, R_eq = R\u2081 + R\u2082 + R\u2083 = l/(2KA) + l/(KA) + l/(2KA) = 4l/(2KA)
R_eq = 2l/(KA)
In series the rate of heat flow is the same.
(3T \u2212 T\u2081)/R\u2081 = (3T \u2212 T)/R_eq
(3T \u2212 T\u2081)(2KA)/l = (2T)(KA)/(2l)
6T \u2212 2T\u2081 = T
2T\u2081 = 5T
T\u2081 = 5T/2 ...(1)
Now equate the heat flow in the 3rd section and the total.
(T\u2082 \u2212 T)/R\u2083 = (3T \u2212 T)/R_eq
(T\u2082 \u2212 T)(2KA)/l = 2T(KA)/(2l)
2T\u2082 \u2212 2T = T
T\u2082 = 3T/2 ...(2)
From (1) and (2): T\u2081/T\u2082 = (5T \xD7 2)/(2 \xD7 3T) = 5/3`,fig:{w:310,h:100,alt:"Three rods in series: 2K, K and 2K, left end wall at 3T and right end wall at T; the junctions are T1 (left) and T2 (right).",d:[["r",49.4,20.9,204.3,46.6,""],["l",115.9,20.9,115.9,67.5,""],["l",184.3,20.9,184.3,67.5,""],["l",48.4,5.7,48.4,88.4,"B"],["l",253.7,5.7,253.7,88.4,"B"],["l",36.1,20.9,48.4,12.4,""],["l",253.7,20.9,266,12.4,""],["l",36.1,28.5,48.4,20,""],["l",253.7,28.5,266,20,""],["l",36.1,36.1,48.4,27.6,""],["l",253.7,36.1,266,27.6,""],["l",36.1,43.7,48.4,35.2,""],["l",253.7,43.7,266,35.2,""],["l",36.1,51.3,48.4,42.8,""],["l",253.7,51.3,266,42.8,""],["l",36.1,58.9,48.4,50.3,""],["l",253.7,58.9,266,50.3,""],["l",36.1,66.5,48.4,58,""],["l",253.7,66.5,266,58,""],["l",36.1,74.1,48.4,65.6,""],["l",253.7,74.1,266,65.6,""],["l",36.1,81.7,48.4,73.1,""],["l",253.7,81.7,266,73.1,""],["l",36.1,89.3,48.4,80.8,""],["l",253.7,89.3,266,80.8,""],["t",11.4,49.4,"3T",""],["t",77,49.4,"2K","m"],["t",150.1,49.4,"K","m"],["t",216.6,49.4,"2K","m"],["t",275.5,49.4,"T",""],["t",116.9,87.4,"T\u2081","m"],["t",184.3,87.4,"T\u2082","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q041",pn:41,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Heat conduction in series",q:"Two rods one made of copper and other made of steel of the same length and same cross sectional area are joined together. The thermal conductivity of copper and steel are 385 J s\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9m\u207B\xB9 and 50 J s\u207B\xB9K\u207B\xB9m\u207B\xB9 respectively. The free ends of copper and steel are held at 100\xB0C and 0\xB0C respectively. The temperature at the junction is, nearly:",o:["88.5\xB0C","12\xB0C","50\xB0C","73\xB0C"],c:0,s:`In steady state the heat flow in both rods is equal.
Let the junction temperature be \u03B8. Then K_Cu A(100 \u2212 \u03B8)/l = K_steel A(\u03B8 \u2212 0)/l.
385(100 \u2212 \u03B8) = 50\u03B8.
38500 = 435\u03B8, so \u03B8 \u2248 88.5\xB0C.`,fig:{w:256,h:96,alt:"A copper rod and a steel rod joined end to end: free ends at 100 degrees C and 0 degrees C, junction at theta degrees C, heat flowing left to right as H1 and H2.",d:[["r",8,28,240,24],["l",128,28,128,52],["t",68,20,"Cu","m"],["t",188,20,"Steel","m"],["t",8,72,"100\xB0C"],["l",54,69,100,69,"a"],["t",77,86,"H\u2081","m"],["t",128,72,"\u03B8\xB0C","m"],["l",144,69,190,69,"a"],["t",167,86,"H\u2082","m"],["t",248,72,"0\xB0C","e"]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Newton's law of cooling",q:"A cup of coffee cools from 90\xB0C to 80\xB0C in t minutes, when the room temperature is 20\xB0C. The time taken by a similar cup of coffee to cool from 80\xB0C to 60\xB0C at a room temperature same at 20\xB0C is",o:["(5)/(13) t","(13)/(10) t","(13)/(5) t","(10)/(13) t"],c:2,s:`Use the average form of Newton's law: (T1 \u2013 T2)/\u0394t = K[(T1 + T2)/2 \u2013 Ts].
Case 1: 10/t = K(85 \u2013 20) = 65K, so K = 2/(13t).
Case 2: 20/t\u2081 = K(70 \u2013 20) = 50K = 100/(13t).
t\u2081 = 20 \xD7 13t/100 = 13t/5.`},{id:"neet-2020-q132",pn:132,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Heat capacity",q:"The quantities of heat required to raise the temperature of two solid copper spheres of radii r\u2081 and r\u2082 (r\u2081 = 1.5 r\u2082) through 1 K are in the ratio :",o:["9/4","3/2","5/3","27/8"],c:3,s:`Heat Q = ms\u0394T, and s and \u0394T are the same.
So Q \u221D m = (4/3)\u03C0r\xB3\u03C1 \u221D r\xB3.
Q\u2081/Q\u2082 = (r\u2081/r\u2082)\xB3 = (1.5)\xB3 = 27/8.`},{id:"neet-2019-q108",pn:108,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal conductivity",q:"The unit of thermal conductivity is :",o:["W m K\u207B\xB9","W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9","J m K^b 1","J m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Heat current H = kA\u0394\u03B8/\u2113, so k = H\u2113/(A\u0394\u03B8).
Unit = W\xB7m/(m\xB2\xB7K) = W m\u207B\xB9 K\u207B\xB9.
Note: Option 3 is garbled in the source (printed 'J m K^b 1'); written as printed.`},{id:"neet-2019-q135",pn:135,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Thermal expansion",q:"A copper rod of 88 cm and an aluminium rod of unknown length have their increase in length independent of increase in temperature. The length of aluminium rod is (\u03B1Cu = 1.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9 and \u03B1Al = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 K\u207B\xB9)",o:["88 cm","68 cm","6.8 cm","113.9 cm"],c:1,s:`The increase in length is the same for both rods at any temperature change.
\u0394\u2113 = \u2113\u03B1\u0394T, so \u2113\u2081\u03B1\u2081 = \u2113\u2082\u03B1\u2082.
88 \xD7 1.7\xD710\u207B\u2075 = \u2113 \xD7 2.2\xD710\u207B\u2075.
\u2113 = 88 \xD7 1.7/2.2 = 68 cm.
Note: The printed comment says the question should read 'difference in length' instead of 'increase in length'.`},{id:"neet-2018-q011",pn:11,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Thermal Properties of Matter",st:"Wien's law and Stefan's law",q:"The power radiated by a black body is P and it radiates maximum energy at wavelength, \u03BB\u2080. If the temperature of the black body is now changed so that it radiates maximum energy at wavelength (3)/(4)\u03BB\u2080, the power radiated by it becomes nP. The value of n is",o:["(3)/(4)","(4)/(3)","(256)/(81)","(81)/(256)"],c:2,s:`Stefan's law: power \u221D T\u2074.
Wien's law: \u03BBT = constant, so T \u221D (1)/(\u03BB).
Hence power \u221D (1)/(\u03BB\u2074).
(nP)/(P) = (\u03BB\u2080/(3\u03BB\u2080/4))\u2074 = ((4)/(3))\u2074 = (256)/(81).
So n = (256)/(81).`}]})();
